{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Parallelism=2 but Split List Ignored",
    "Explanation": [
      "The job config sets parallelism: 2, which spins up two parallel containers (CIRCLE_NODE_INDEX 0 and 1, CIRCLE_NODE_TOTAL=2). The script does call `circleci tests split --split-by=name`, which correctly computes a different subset of test files for each node (node 0 gets test_goodbye.sh, node 1 gets test_hello.sh) and writes it to /tmp/split.txt. However, the actual test execution loop `for f in src/test_*.sh; do ... bash \"$f\"; done` does not reference /tmp/split.txt at all — it iterates over *all* matching test files regardless of node index. As the log shows, both node 0 and node 1 ran both test_goodbye.sh and test_hello.sh (Ran 2 file(s)), while the split list only expected 1 file per node. This mismatch triggers the explicit check `[ \"$RAN\" -ne \"$EXPECTED\" ]`, causing both containers to exit with code 1 and fail the build. So while CircleCI's split mechanism correctly partitioned the suite, the workflow never consumed that partition — each node redundantly executed the entire test suite instead of just its assigned slice. The fix is not to touch the workflow/job topology (so options like increasing parallelism, splitting into separate workflow jobs, or dropping parallelism don't address the root cause), but to actually use the generated split file so each node only runs the tests assigned to it via `circleci tests split`."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
      "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
      "  \"suite_split\": true",
      "}"
    ],
    "ExpectedAnswer": [
      [
        "{",
        "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
        "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
        "  \"suite_split\": false",
        "}"
      ]
